# ntfy 2.28.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins binwiederhier/ntfy:v2.28.0 (docker.io digest
# sha256:6ef4b819f722fccdc036af611c4774cfdc2de821ab74fdd48bbf4c9d6f8973da,
# pushed 2026-08-27, the same digest `latest`/`v2`/`v2.28` currently point
# at; the tag was floating before this pin).
#
# Source: the flag definitions in cmd/serve.go (lines 35-122) and cmd/app.go
# (28-34) at tag v2.28.0, plus the constants they default from in
# server/config.go, server/config_unix.go and user/manager.go. ntfy is Go and
# server.yml is the documented config surface, but every single config option
# is declared with `EnvVars: []string{"NTFY_..."}` on the same urfave/cli
# flag, so each YAML key has an exact env twin (upstream says so in
# docs/config.md: "you can set an environment variable before running the
# ntfy command"). Diffing the 95 NTFY_* names in the code against the 80 in
# the docs table found:
#   - The docs table lists `NTFY_CACHE_BATCH_SIZE` for `cache-batch-size`.
#     The code reads `NTFY_BATCH_SIZE` (cmd/serve.go:48), and the CLI help
#     pasted further down the SAME docs page agrees ([$NTFY_BATCH_SIZE]).
#     NTFY_CACHE_BATCH_SIZE is documented but never read; it is not declared
#     below.
#   - The docs table omits the env twins of NTFY_AUTH_USERS,
#     NTFY_AUTH_ACCESS, NTFY_AUTH_TOKENS, NTFY_AUTH_STARTUP_QUERIES,
#     NTFY_DISALLOWED_TOPICS, NTFY_TEMPLATE_DIR, NTFY_TWILIO_CALL_FORMAT,
#     NTFY_ENABLE_METRICS, NTFY_METRICS_LISTEN_HTTP,
#     NTFY_PROFILE_LISTEN_HTTP, NTFY_LOG_LEVEL_OVERRIDES, NTFY_DEBUG,
#     NTFY_TRACE and NTFY_CONFIG_FILE even though the code reads all of them.
#
# ─── READ THIS FIRST: ntfy's default auth posture is OPEN ─────────────────
# ntfy has no accounts, no sessions and no authorization at all until you
# give it an auth database. With `auth-file` unset, server_auth.go:32 short
# circuits (`if s.userManager == nil { return r, vip, nil }`): every request
# is anonymous and allowed. And even WITH an auth file, the shipped default
# for auth-default-access is `read-write` (cmd/serve.go:53), so the anonymous
# "everyone" user may publish to and subscribe to every topic.
#
# Topics are the only namespace. There is no per-topic secret, no hidden
# topic, no listing protection worth relying on: anyone who can reach the
# base URL and guesses (or observes, or brute-forces) a topic name can read
# every message on it and inject their own. A long random topic name is
# obscurity, not access control.
#
# The real control is NTFY_AUTH_FILE + NTFY_AUTH_DEFAULT_ACCESS=deny-all,
# then explicit grants. This compose already sets both (auth-file at
# /var/lib/ntfy/auth.db on a persistent volume, and deny-all as the default
# for NTFY_AUTH_DEFAULT_ACCESS), so a fresh deploy is closed. It is also
# EMPTY: deny-all with no users means nobody can publish or subscribe until
# you create them. That step is not an environment variable. Exec into the
# container and use the CLI, which writes to the same auth.db:
#     ntfy user add --role=admin alice          # or --role=user
#     ntfy access bob 'alerts*' rw              # per-topic ACL entry
#     ntfy access everyone announcements read   # anonymous read-only topic
#     ntfy token add alice                      # bearer token for scripts
# v2.28.0 also accepts pre-provisioned users/ACLs/tokens declaratively via
# NTFY_AUTH_USERS / NTFY_AUTH_ACCESS / NTFY_AUTH_TOKENS (see those keys), an
# alternative to the CLI that survives a volume reset, at the cost of
# putting a bcrypt hash and raw tokens in the environment.
#
# Other things that change the security posture, all off by default and all
# called out again at their keys: NTFY_ENABLE_LOGIN / NTFY_ENABLE_SIGNUP
# (self-service accounts), NTFY_AUTH_DEFAULT_ACCESS set to anything other
# than deny-all, and every rate-limit / attachment-limit knob in the
# "Rate limits" and "Attachments" sections, which exist to bound what one
# anonymous visitor can cost you.
#
# One correctness trap worth repeating: NTFY_BASE_URL must be the exact
# externally visible origin. It is not cosmetic: it is baked into the
# attachment URLs and click/action URLs ntfy hands to clients, so a wrong
# value produces messages whose attachments and buttons point somewhere
# unreachable. The server also refuses to start without it here, because the
# compose sets an attachment cache dir and cmd/serve.go:349 requires
# base-url whenever attachment-cache-dir is set.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("ntfy server config options", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#config-options)
# ---

# ─── Core ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The externally visible base URL of this instance, scheme included, no
# trailing path (ntfy refuses a URL with a path; sub-path hosting is not
# supported). It is what ntfy writes into attachment URLs and click/action
# URLs, and what the web app and mobile clients are told to talk to. The
# compose sets NTFY_ATTACHMENT_CACHE_DIR unconditionally, and ntfy errors out
# at startup if attachment-cache-dir is set without base-url, so on this
# template it is genuinely mandatory, not optional.
# @required @type=url
# @docs("Behind a proxy (TLS, etc.)", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#behind-a-proxy-tls-etc)
NTFY_BASE_URL=

# ip:port the HTTP server binds to. The compose exposes container port 80
# and does not override this, so changing it here without changing the
# exposed port breaks routing. "-" is a special value that disables the HTTP
# listener entirely.
NTFY_LISTEN_HTTP=:80

# Terminate TLS inside the container instead of at the platform's proxy. If
# set, NTFY_KEY_FILE and NTFY_CERT_FILE must both be set and must exist
# inside the container. Normally leave empty: otterdeploy already terminates
# TLS in front of the service.
# @docs("TLS/SSL", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#tlsssl)
NTFY_LISTEN_HTTPS=
# Private key file for NTFY_LISTEN_HTTPS. Must exist at startup or ntfy
# refuses to boot.
NTFY_KEY_FILE=
# Certificate file for NTFY_LISTEN_HTTPS. Must exist at startup or ntfy
# refuses to boot.
NTFY_CERT_FILE=

# Additionally listen on a unix socket at this path. Not supported on
# Windows. Rarely useful in a container.
NTFY_LISTEN_UNIX=
# Octal file permissions for the unix socket, e.g. 0700. Empty leaves the
# system default.
NTFY_LISTEN_UNIX_MODE=

# Serves the web app from a sub-path (e.g. /app), or disables it entirely
# with the literal "disable", worth doing on a publish-only instance, since
# the web app is the thing that makes an open topic trivially browsable.
# "app" and "home" are legacy aliases for "/" and "/app".
NTFY_WEB_ROOT=/

# Topic names that may never be used, appended to ntfy's own built-in list
# (docs, static, file, app, metrics, account, settings, signup, login, v1).
# Comma-separated.
NTFY_DISALLOWED_TOPICS=

# Path to an alternate server.yml. The compose configures ntfy purely
# through NTFY_* variables and ships no config file, so this stays at the
# image default; a file at that path, if you mount one, is read as a lower
# priority source than these variables.
NTFY_CONFIG_FILE=/etc/ntfy/server.yml

# ─── Access control ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The SQLite auth database. Everything about authentication and
# authorization is off until this is set: with no auth file ntfy skips
# authorization entirely and treats every request as an allowed anonymous
# one. The compose sets it to a path on the ntfy-data volume, so users and
# ACLs survive redeploys. Changing it to an empty value re-opens the
# instance completely.
# @docs("Access control", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#access-control)
NTFY_AUTH_FILE=/var/lib/ntfy/auth.db

# What the anonymous "everyone" user may do on a topic with no matching ACL
# entry. ntfy's own default is `read-write`, i.e. wide open; this compose
# overrides it to `deny-all`, so nothing works until you grant access with
# `ntfy access` (or NTFY_AUTH_ACCESS below). `read-only` lets anyone
# subscribe but only authorized users publish; `write-only` is the inverse
# (useful for a drop-box instance). Anything other than deny-all means
# unauthenticated strangers can use your instance.
# @type=enum(read-write, read-only, write-only, deny-all)
# @docs("Access control list (ACL)", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#access-control-list-acl)
NTFY_AUTH_DEFAULT_ACCESS=deny-all

# Pre-provisioned users, comma-separated, each `name:bcrypt-hash:role` with
# role `admin` or `user`. Created or updated at every startup and marked
# provisioned, so they cannot be deleted from the CLI. Generate the hash
# with `ntfy user hash`; an invalid hash or role aborts startup. Convenient
# for a reproducible deploy, but it puts a password hash in the environment.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Users via the config", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#users-via-the-config)
NTFY_AUTH_USERS=

# Pre-provisioned ACL entries, comma-separated, each `user:topic:permission`
# where user is a name from NTFY_AUTH_USERS or the literal `everyone`
# (`*` also works), topic may use `*` wildcards, and permission is
# read-write / read-only / write-only / deny-all (rw / ro / wo / deny).
# Every named user must be provisioned in NTFY_AUTH_USERS and must have role
# `user` (admins already have everything) or startup fails.
# @docs("ACL entries via the config", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#acl-entries-via-the-cli)
NTFY_AUTH_ACCESS=

# Pre-provisioned access tokens, comma-separated, each `user:token[:label]`.
# The user must appear in NTFY_AUTH_USERS. Generate a well-formed token with
# `ntfy token generate`. These are bearer credentials in plaintext in the
# environment. Prefer `ntfy token add` unless you need the deploy to be
# reproducible from config alone.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Tokens via the config", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#tokens-via-the-config)
NTFY_AUTH_TOKENS=

# Lets people create their own accounts through the web app or API. Off by
# default, and it requires NTFY_ENABLE_LOGIN as well (ntfy refuses to start
# with signup on and login off). On a private instance leave this off:
# turning it on makes the instance self-service to anyone who can reach it.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_ENABLE_SIGNUP=false

# Lets existing users log in through the web app or API. Off by default.
# Requires an auth file (or NTFY_DATABASE_URL); ntfy refuses to start if
# login/signup/reservations/Stripe are enabled with neither set.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_ENABLE_LOGIN=false

# Requires a login for every action in the web app. Only meaningful with
# NTFY_ENABLE_LOGIN=true (startup fails otherwise). Note this hardens the
# WEB APP; the HTTP API is still governed by the ACL, so deny-all above is
# what actually closes the door.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_REQUIRE_LOGIN=false

# Lets users claim ("reserve") a topic and control who else may use it, if
# their tier allows it. Requires an auth file (or NTFY_DATABASE_URL).
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Tiers", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#tiers)
NTFY_ENABLE_RESERVATIONS=false

# Keeps resolved ACL decisions in memory instead of hitting the auth
# database per request. Upstream marks it for high-volume servers only; a
# permission change can take effect late while an entry is cached.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_AUTH_ACCESS_CACHE=false

# SQL statements run against the auth database right after it is opened
# (e.g. `pragma journal_mode = WAL;`). A tuning escape hatch, not a place
# for schema changes.
# @docs("Tuning for scale", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#tuning-for-scale)
NTFY_AUTH_STARTUP_QUERIES=

# ─── Message cache ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SQLite file holding cached messages, which is what makes `since=` polling
# and app catch-up work. The compose puts it on the ntfy-cache volume. The
# literal value `-` keeps the cache in memory only (lost on restart); an
# empty value disables caching entirely, so subscribers see only what
# arrives while they are connected.
# @docs("Message cache", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#message-cache)
NTFY_CACHE_FILE=/var/cache/ntfy/cache.db

# How long messages stay in the cache and remain replayable. 0 disables
# caching. Must not be lower than NTFY_MANAGER_INTERVAL.
NTFY_CACHE_DURATION=12h

# Batch this many message writes together instead of writing synchronously.
# 0 (the default) means synchronous. Note the env twin is NTFY_BATCH_SIZE,
# NOT the NTFY_CACHE_BATCH_SIZE printed in the docs option table.
# @type=number
NTFY_BATCH_SIZE=

# How long a partial batch waits before being flushed. 0s means synchronous
# writes; only meaningful together with NTFY_BATCH_SIZE.
NTFY_CACHE_BATCH_TIMEOUT=0s

# SQL statements run against the message-cache database right after it is
# opened. Same escape hatch as NTFY_AUTH_STARTUP_QUERIES.
NTFY_CACHE_STARTUP_QUERIES=

# How often ntfy prunes expired messages and prints stats. Must be at least
# 5s, and NTFY_CACHE_DURATION must not be lower than this.
NTFY_MANAGER_INTERVAL=1m

# How often the server sends a keepalive down an open subscription so
# proxies do not drop it. Must be at least 5s.
NTFY_KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL=45s

# ─── PostgreSQL backend (alternative to the SQLite files) ─────────────────

# Puts the auth database, message cache and web-push subscriptions in
# PostgreSQL instead of SQLite files. MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the file
# paths: if this is set, ntfy refuses to start unless NTFY_AUTH_FILE,
# NTFY_CACHE_FILE and NTFY_WEB_PUSH_FILE are all empty, and this compose
# sets the first two, so switching to Postgres means blanking them here as
# well. Must start with postgres:// or postgresql://.
# @sensitive
# @docs("PostgreSQL", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#postgresql)
NTFY_DATABASE_URL=

# Comma-separated read-replica connection strings that read queries are
# offloaded to. Only usable when NTFY_DATABASE_URL is also set.
# @sensitive
NTFY_DATABASE_REPLICA_URLS=

# ─── Attachments ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where uploaded attachments are stored. A filesystem path (the compose uses
# the ntfy-cache volume), or an S3 URL of the form
# s3://ACCESS_KEY:SECRET_KEY@BUCKET[/PREFIX]?region=REGION[&endpoint=...].
# Setting this REQUIRES NTFY_BASE_URL: ntfy will not start without it,
# because attachment URLs are built from the base URL.
# @docs("Attachments", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#attachments)
NTFY_ATTACHMENT_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/ntfy/attachments

# Ceiling on the whole attachment store. Raising it raises how much disk an
# anonymous publisher can consume on a wide-open instance.
NTFY_ATTACHMENT_TOTAL_SIZE_LIMIT=5G

# Per-file upload ceiling. This is one of the two limits (with the visitor
# quotas below) standing between an open instance and someone using it as
# free file hosting.
NTFY_ATTACHMENT_FILE_SIZE_LIMIT=15M

# How long an uploaded attachment is kept before deletion. Longer retention
# means more disk held per upload.
NTFY_ATTACHMENT_EXPIRY_DURATION=3h

# ─── Message limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Max message body size. Values above 4K log a warning ("not recommended and
# largely untested"), and anything above 5M is rejected at startup. Note
# v2.28.0 additionally caps titles at 1 KB and all tags combined at 512
# bytes; those two are hard-coded and have no config option.
NTFY_MESSAGE_SIZE_LIMIT=4K

# How far into the future a message may be scheduled with the Delay header.
NTFY_MESSAGE_DELAY_LIMIT=3d

# Hard ceiling on the number of topics the server will hold at once.
# @type=number
NTFY_GLOBAL_TOPIC_LIMIT=15000

# Directory of named message templates loadable by `template=<name>`. The
# image ships /etc/ntfy/templates; point this at a mounted directory to add
# your own.
# @docs("Message templating", https://docs.ntfy.sh/publish/#message-templating)
NTFY_TEMPLATE_DIR=/etc/ntfy/templates

# ─── Behind a proxy ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Tells ntfy to trust a forwarded header for the visitor IP instead of the
# TCP peer address. The compose sets it to true because otterdeploy always
# runs ntfy behind its own proxy. Without it EVERY request rate-limits as
# one visitor. It is also the flip side of that: with it on, whoever can
# reach ntfy directly can forge the header and evade rate limits, so pair it
# with NTFY_PROXY_TRUSTED_HOSTS if the container is reachable off-proxy.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Behind a proxy (TLS, etc.)", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#behind-a-proxy-tls-etc)
NTFY_BEHIND_PROXY=true

# Which header carries the visitor IP. Must be non-empty whenever
# NTFY_BEHIND_PROXY is true, or ntfy refuses to start. X-Client-IP,
# X-Real-IP and Forwarded are the other common choices.
NTFY_PROXY_FORWARDED_HEADER=X-Forwarded-For

# Comma-separated IPs, hostnames or CIDRs to strip from the right of the
# forwarded header before taking the visitor IP, i.e. your own proxy hops.
# Empty means ntfy takes the leftmost entry, which a client can spoof.
NTFY_PROXY_TRUSTED_HOSTS=

# ─── Rate limits and abuse ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every value here is a bound on what ONE visitor (one IP, or one account)
# can cost. On an instance that is not deny-all, they are the only thing
# limiting an anonymous stranger. Raise them deliberately.

# Max concurrent subscriptions per visitor.
# @type=number
# @docs("Rate limiting", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#rate-limiting)
NTFY_VISITOR_SUBSCRIPTION_LIMIT=30

# Request token bucket: the burst size a visitor may spend at once.
# @type=number
NTFY_VISITOR_REQUEST_LIMIT_BURST=60

# How fast the request bucket refills: one token per this interval.
NTFY_VISITOR_REQUEST_LIMIT_REPLENISH=5s

# Comma-separated hosts/IPs exempt from the request limit, resolved at
# startup. An unresolvable entry is warned about and skipped, not fatal.
# Everything listed here is effectively unthrottled.
NTFY_VISITOR_REQUEST_LIMIT_EXEMPT_HOSTS=

# Absolute messages-per-visitor-per-day cap. 0 means "derive it from the
# request limit" rather than "unlimited".
# @type=number
NTFY_VISITOR_MESSAGE_DAILY_LIMIT=0

# Token bucket for new-topic creation per visitor; 0 disables the limit.
# @type=number
# @docs("Topic creation limits", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#topic-creation-limits)
NTFY_VISITOR_TOPIC_CREATION_LIMIT_BURST=100

# Refill interval for the topic-creation bucket.
NTFY_VISITOR_TOPIC_CREATION_LIMIT_REPLENISH=1m

# Total attachment storage one visitor may hold at a time.
NTFY_VISITOR_ATTACHMENT_TOTAL_SIZE_LIMIT=100M

# Daily traffic budget per visitor. As of v2.28.0 this covers BOTH
# attachment upload/download AND messages replayed out of the message cache
# by poll requests (a poll without `since` returns a topic's whole cache, so
# a cheap-to-fill topic could be re-read for many times its own size). Over
# budget returns HTTP 429 (code 42905). Heavy pollers now share this budget
# with attachment traffic, so operators serving both may need to raise it.
NTFY_VISITOR_ATTACHMENT_DAILY_BANDWIDTH_LIMIT=500M

# Rate-limits by SUBSCRIBER instead of publisher for topics that have a
# rate-limit subscriber, so a webhook source publishing from many IPs is not
# throttled per source.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Subscriber-based rate limiting", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#subscriber-based-rate-limiting)
NTFY_VISITOR_SUBSCRIBER_RATE_LIMITING=false

# How many bits of an IPv4 address identify a "visitor". 32 (the default) is
# the full address; lowering it groups a subnet into one bucket, which stops
# someone cycling addresses inside a range. Must be 1-32.
# @type=number
# @docs("IPv6 considerations", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#ipv6-considerations)
NTFY_VISITOR_PREFIX_BITS_IPV4=32

# Same for IPv6; 64 (a /64) is the default because a single client is
# routinely handed far more than one IPv6 address. Must be 1-128.
# @type=number
NTFY_VISITOR_PREFIX_BITS_IPV6=64

# Append the IPs of abusive visitors to this file for fail2ban to tail.
# Empty (the default) disables the feed. The file's DIRECTORY must already
# exist inside the container or ntfy refuses to start, and ban-window,
# ban-threshold and ban-weights must all be non-empty.
# @docs("Ban feed", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#ban-feed)
NTFY_BAN_FILE=

# Rolling window over which weighted strikes are counted for the ban feed.
NTFY_BAN_WINDOW=10m

# Weighted strikes within one window before an offender is written to the
# ban file.
# @type=number
NTFY_BAN_THRESHOLD=100

# Per-code strike weights, comma-separated `KEY:WEIGHT` where KEY is an ntfy
# error code, an HTTP status, a `PREFIX*`, or `*`; weight 0 exempts. The
# default weights the auth-failure limit (42909) at 10 so a brute-force
# flood bans fast, and everything else at 1.
NTFY_BAN_WEIGHTS=42909:10

# ─── E-mail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Outbound SMTP server as host:port, which turns on the "email" publish
# option. Requires NTFY_BASE_URL and NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_FROM to also be set,
# or ntfy will not start.
# @docs("E-mail notifications", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#e-mail-notifications)
NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_ADDR=
NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_USER=
# @sensitive
NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_PASS=
# From address on outgoing mail. Required whenever NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_ADDR is
# set.
# @type=email
NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_FROM=
# Only let users send email notifications to addresses they have verified.
# Requires NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_ADDR. Without it, an authorized publisher can
# make your SMTP server send mail to any address, i.e. relay through you.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_SMTP_SENDER_VERIFY=false

# ip:port for ntfy's own INBOUND SMTP server, which turns email into
# published messages (e.g. :25). Requires NTFY_SMTP_SERVER_DOMAIN. This
# opens another unauthenticated ingress path onto your topics, governed by
# the address prefix below rather than by the ACL.
# @docs("E-mail publishing", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#e-mail-publishing)
NTFY_SMTP_SERVER_LISTEN=
# The mail domain incoming addresses are matched against, e.g. ntfy.sh.
# Mandatory whenever NTFY_SMTP_SERVER_LISTEN is set.
NTFY_SMTP_SERVER_DOMAIN=
# Required prefix on the local part of an incoming address (e.g. "ntfy-", so
# only ntfy-mytopic@domain publishes). Empty means every topic is reachable
# by mail; set it if you expose the SMTP listener.
NTFY_SMTP_SERVER_ADDR_PREFIX=

# Token bucket for emails sent per visitor.
# @type=number
# @docs("E-mail limits", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#e-mail-limits)
NTFY_VISITOR_EMAIL_LIMIT_BURST=16
# Refill interval for the email bucket.
NTFY_VISITOR_EMAIL_LIMIT_REPLENISH=1h

# ─── Phone calls (Twilio) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Twilio account SID (AC...), which enables voice-call notifications. If
# set, the auth token, phone number, verify service, NTFY_BASE_URL and an
# auth file (or NTFY_DATABASE_URL) must all be set too, or startup fails.
# @docs("Phone calls", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#phone-calls)
NTFY_TWILIO_ACCOUNT=
# @sensitive
NTFY_TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# The Twilio number outgoing calls originate from, in E.164 form.
NTFY_TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=
# Twilio Verify service ID, used to verify a user's phone number before it
# can be called.
NTFY_TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE=
# Go text/template producing the TwiML for a call. Parsed at startup; a
# malformed template aborts the boot.
NTFY_TWILIO_CALL_FORMAT=

# ─── Web push (browser notifications) ─────────────────────────────────────

# VAPID public key. Setting it turns web push on, and then the private key,
# NTFY_WEB_PUSH_FILE (or NTFY_DATABASE_URL), NTFY_WEB_PUSH_EMAIL_ADDRESS and
# NTFY_BASE_URL must all be set as well. Generate a pair with
# `ntfy webpush keys`.
# @docs("Web Push", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#web-push)
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID private key from the same `ntfy webpush keys` pair. Anyone holding
# it can push to your subscribers.
# @sensitive
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_PRIVATE_KEY=
# SQLite file storing browser push subscriptions. Must be empty if
# NTFY_DATABASE_URL is set. Put it on the ntfy-data volume if you enable web
# push, or subscriptions vanish on redeploy.
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_FILE=
# Contact address sent to Google/Mozilla/Apple push services as the VAPID
# subject; they require one. It is visible to those services.
# @type=email
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_EMAIL_ADDRESS=
# SQL statements run against the web-push database right after it is opened.
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_STARTUP_QUERIES=
# Unused browser subscriptions are expired after this long.
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_EXPIRY_DURATION=60d
# How long before expiry a warning notification is sent. Must not exceed
# NTFY_WEB_PUSH_EXPIRY_DURATION or ntfy refuses to start.
NTFY_WEB_PUSH_EXPIRY_WARNING_DURATION=55d

# ─── iOS instant notifications (upstream) ─────────────────────────────────

# Forwards a poll to an upstream ntfy server, which is what makes instant
# notifications work for the iOS app against a self-hosted instance,
# normally https://ntfy.sh. Must start with http(s)://, must not end in a
# slash, requires NTFY_BASE_URL, and must differ from it. Only a message ID
# is forwarded, but the upstream does learn that a message exists.
# @type=url
# @docs("iOS instant notifications", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#ios-instant-notifications)
NTFY_UPSTREAM_BASE_URL=
# Token for the upstream server, needed only if you exceed its rate limits
# or it requires auth.
# @sensitive
NTFY_UPSTREAM_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ─── Firebase (Android FCM) ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Path to a Firebase service-account JSON inside the container; if set, ntfy
# also publishes to FCM. The file must exist at startup. Only relevant if
# you ship your own Android build, since the Play Store app talks to ntfy.sh's
# Firebase project, not yours.
# @docs("Firebase (FCM)", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#firebase-fcm)
NTFY_FIREBASE_KEY_FILE=

# ─── Payments (Stripe) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Enables paid tiers. Requires the webhook key and NTFY_BASE_URL, plus an
# auth file (or NTFY_DATABASE_URL). Only meaningful if you are selling
# access to this instance.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Payments", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#payments)
NTFY_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Signing secret used to validate incoming Stripe webhooks. Mandatory
# whenever the secret key is set.
# @sensitive
NTFY_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_KEY=
# Email address or URL shown in the upgrade dialog. Only used when payments
# are enabled.
NTFY_BILLING_CONTACT=

# ─── Monitoring, profiling, logging ───────────────────────────────────────

# Exposes Prometheus metrics at /metrics on the main HTTP listener, i.e.
# publicly, on the same port the platform routes to, with no auth of its
# own. Prefer NTFY_METRICS_LISTEN_HTTP on a separate unpublished port.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Monitoring", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#monitoring)
NTFY_ENABLE_METRICS=false
# ip:port for a dedicated metrics listener. Setting it implicitly enables
# metrics; the compose publishes only port 80, so a port chosen here stays
# inside the container network unless you expose it.
NTFY_METRICS_LISTEN_HTTP=

# ip:port exposing Go's pprof endpoints. Setting it implicitly enables
# profiling. Never point this at a published port: pprof will dump heap and
# goroutine state to anyone who asks.
# @docs("Profiling", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#profiling)
NTFY_PROFILE_LISTEN_HTTP=

# Log verbosity. TRACE is extremely verbose and logs request contents.
# @type=enum(TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, FATAL)
# @docs("Logging and debugging", https://docs.ntfy.sh/config/#logging-debugging)
NTFY_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# Per-field level overrides, comma-separated, in the form
# `field=value -> level` (e.g. `tag=manager -> trace`), for turning up one
# subsystem without drowning in TRACE globally.
NTFY_LOG_LEVEL_OVERRIDES=
# `json` emits structured log lines; anything else is the human-readable
# text format.
# @type=enum(text, json)
NTFY_LOG_FORMAT=text
# Write logs to this file instead of stdout. In a container leave it empty
# so the platform collects the logs.
NTFY_LOG_FILE=
# Shorthand for NTFY_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_DEBUG=false
# Shorthand for NTFY_LOG_LEVEL=TRACE. Upstream's own warning: "very verbose,
# be careful". It logs message contents.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_TRACE=false
# Drops the date/time prefix from text log lines, for when the log collector
# adds its own timestamp.
# @type=boolean
NTFY_NO_LOG_DATES=false

# ─── Timezone ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Standard Go/glibc timezone variable, read by the runtime rather than by
# ntfy's own config. Affects log timestamps and how scheduled-delivery times
# are rendered. The compose defaults it to UTC.
TZ=UTC
